This page carries the parts of the readout that a reader only wants on demand: what
each column is, how the file was generated, and whether the model in
[the readout](index.tmd) is one you should believe.

## The dataset {#sec-fields}

One row per region-week-channel. Thirteen ISO weeks, three regions, two channels, so
78 rows and no gaps: every combination is present, including the week-33 rows that the
readout excludes.

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|--------|------|---------|
| `week` | integer | ISO week number, 27 through 39 |
| `region` | text | `eu-west`, `us-east`, or `ap-south` |
| `channel` | text | `stable` (about 88% of traffic) or `canary` (about 12%) |
| `requests_k` | float | Requests served that week, in thousands |
| `p50_ms` | float | Median queue time in milliseconds |
| `p95_ms` | float | 95th-percentile queue time in milliseconds |
| `errors` | integer | Failed dequeues that week, as a count |

: Every column in `data/latency.csv`. {#tbl-fields}

`p50_ms` and `p95_ms` are percentiles of the *queue* time, the interval between a
request being enqueued and a worker picking it up. They exclude the work itself, which
is why a p95 near 150 ms is a meaningful objective rather than an alarming one.

## How the file was generated {#sec-generating}

The dataset is synthetic. It is written by the script below, which fixes a seed and
rounds every column, so re-running it reproduces `data/latency.csv` byte for byte. The
script is shown rather than executed: the CSV is committed, and a report that
regenerates its own input on every build would quietly hide a change to the input.

The generating process is not neutral, and saying so is the point of this section. It
has a quarter-long downward drift built into it, a canary penalty that decays to zero
by week 39, and a week-33 incident in `ap-south`. Those are exactly the three effects
the readout goes looking for. Treat the readout as a demonstration that the analysis
recovers a known signal, not as evidence about any real service.

```python
import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(20260726)

WEEKS = list(range(27, 40))       # Q3, ISO weeks 27-39
REGIONS = {              # (baseline p50 ms, weekly kreq)
    "eu-west":  (42.0, 310.0),
    "us-east":  (38.0, 505.0),
    "ap-south": (61.0, 148.0),
}

rows = []
for week in WEEKS:
    drift = 1.0 - 0.011 * (week - 27)   # caching work, week 27
    for region, (base_p50, base_vol) in REGIONS.items():
        for channel in ("stable", "canary"):
            # Canary starts 24% worse, converging by week 39.
            canary = channel == "canary"
            decay = max(0.0, 1.0 - (week - 27) / 11.0)
            penalty = 1.0 + 0.24 * decay
            p50 = base_p50 * drift * rng.normal(1.0, 0.035)
            p50 = p50 * penalty if canary else p50
            p95 = p50 * (2.40 + rng.normal(0.0, 0.09))
            share = 0.12 if canary else 0.88
            requests = base_vol * share * rng.normal(1.0, 0.06)
            err_rate = 0.0019 if canary else 0.0011
            errors = rng.poisson(requests * 1000 * err_rate)

            if week == 33 and region == "ap-south":
                p50 *= 2.9          # the storage-layer incident
                p95 *= 3.4
                errors = int(errors * 7.5)

            rows.append((week, region, channel,
                         round(requests, 1), round(p50, 1),
                         round(p95, 1), int(errors)))
```

## Model diagnostics {#sec-diagnostics}

The readout fits `log(p95) ~ region + channel + week` by least squares in @sec-model:
the model behind @tbl-coefs, and the reason @fig-p95 is read the way it is. Three
assumptions carry it, and the residuals are where they show.

```{python}
#| label: diag-fit
#| include: false

import matplotlib
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from scipy import stats

# Neutral on both themes; see the readout's setup cell for why the recolour hook
# means only the DATA colours have to survive a theme flip unaided.
INK = "#7d7d7d"
REGION_COLOUR = {"eu-west": "#3b82c4", "us-east": "#c4713b", "ap-south": "#5a9e6f"}

matplotlib.rcParams.update({
    "figure.facecolor": "none",
    "axes.facecolor": "none",
    "savefig.facecolor": "none",
    "savefig.transparent": True,
    "text.color": INK,
    "axes.labelcolor": INK,
    "axes.edgecolor": INK,
    "xtick.color": INK,
    "ytick.color": INK,
    "axes.spines.top": False,
    "axes.spines.right": False,
    "axes.grid": True,
    "grid.color": INK,
    "grid.alpha": 0.22,
    "grid.linewidth": 0.6,
    "font.size": 9,
    "figure.dpi": 150,
})

# This page stands alone: it re-reads the CSV and re-fits rather than reaching for the
# readout's kernel state, because each page gets its own execution.
latency = pd.read_csv("data/latency.csv")
clean = latency[~((latency.week == 33) & (latency.region == "ap-south"))]

design = np.column_stack([
    np.ones(len(clean)),
    (clean.region == "us-east").astype(float).to_numpy(),
    (clean.region == "ap-south").astype(float).to_numpy(),
    (clean.channel == "canary").astype(float).to_numpy(),
    (clean.week - 27).astype(float).to_numpy(),
])
observed = np.log(clean.p95_ms.to_numpy())

beta, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(design, observed, rcond=None)
fitted = design @ beta
residual = observed - fitted
```

```{python}
#| label: fig-residuals
#| fig-cap: "Left: residuals against fitted values, testing constant variance on the log scale. Right: the same residuals as a normal quantile-quantile plot. Both are drawn from the pooled model in the readout."
#| echo: false

fig, (ax_scale, ax_qq) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(7.0, 3.4))

ax_scale.axhline(0, linestyle="--", color=INK, linewidth=0.4)
for region, colour in REGION_COLOUR.items():
    rows = (clean.region == region).to_numpy()
    ax_scale.scatter(fitted[rows], residual[rows], s=8, color=colour, alpha=0.85)
ax_scale.set_title("Residuals vs fitted")
ax_scale.set_xlabel("Fitted log p95")
ax_scale.set_ylabel("Residual")

# The Q-Q line is drawn through the first and third quartiles, which is what R's
# `stat_qq_line` does — a least-squares line through all the points would be pulled
# by the very tails the plot exists to show.
theoretical, sample = stats.probplot(residual, dist="norm", fit=False)
ax_qq.scatter(theoretical, sample, s=8, color="#3b82c4", alpha=0.85)
qx, qy = np.percentile(theoretical, [25, 75]), np.percentile(sample, [25, 75])
slope = (qy[1] - qy[0]) / (qx[1] - qx[0])
ax_qq.plot(theoretical, qy[0] + slope * (theoretical - qx[0]),
           linestyle="--", color=INK, linewidth=0.4)
ax_qq.set_title("Normal Q-Q")
ax_qq.set_xlabel("Theoretical quantile")
ax_qq.set_ylabel("Sample quantile")

fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

@fig-residuals says the log transform did its job: the residual spread is flat across
the fitted range rather than fanning out, which is what it would do if we had fitted
raw milliseconds. The quantile plot is close to the line through the middle and drifts
at both tails, which is ordinary for 76 points and not worth transforming further.

What the residuals cannot tell us is whether the observations are independent. They are
not, quite: consecutive weeks in the same region share whatever the platform was doing
that fortnight, so the standard errors in the readout's coefficient table are, if
anything, a little optimistic. That does not threaten the canary conclusion, which is a
large effect, but it is the reason the readout stops at "measurably slower" rather than
quoting an interval to three decimals.

::: {.callout-note}
## Reproducing this

Both pages read `data/latency.csv` from the project directory. The cells need
`pandas`, `numpy`, `scipy` and `matplotlib`. With those present, `taliesin preview .`
renders the whole report, re-executing only the cells you actually edit.
:::
